Date: Sat, 20 Feb 2016 18:38:12 +0000
Subject: Is it possible to use Weibull likelihood in a Two-Part model in R-INLA?
Message-ID: <_7nd-JasRCc.YHWddcxfCgAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/r-inla-discussion-group/c/_7nd-JasRCc/m/YHWddcxfCgAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hello all,

I work with a Two-Part model with Generalized Gamma distributed non-zero observations and correlated random effects (like one described here <http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC2824028/#FD13>), and analysis shows that the empirical distrbition of non-zero values of my DV can be pretty well approximated by Weibull distribution. I use SAS PROC NLMIXED for estimation, but, given small sample size (both small number of clusters and small cluster size) I also would like to check my inferences using Bayesian approach. I've got how to estimate two-part models that use, for example, gamma, normal, lognormal or skew normal likelihoods for continuous part in R-INLA (one may found basic ideas for INLA implementation here <https://groups.google.com/forum/#!topic/r-inla-discussion-group/VN_qo24Xzx8>; correlated random effects are specified using iid2d model).

But I've also realized that, to use Weibull likelihood, I should specify my model as survival model. However, my data are not indeed "survival". Actually, I'm working with percentages of votes that specific political parties gain in different elections in different countries, so it is not clear how to specify required arguments like time or event for inla.surv(). Without these arguments, however, I cannot use "weibull" likelihood:

                      Error in inla.create.data.file(y.orig = yy, mf = mf, E = E, scale = scale,  :
                            Responce does not contain variable `truncation'.

Is there some trick that might allow for estimation of a two-part model with Weibull likelihood for continuous part?
(according to SAS NLMIXED, TP-models assuming gamma, lognormal or log-skew normal likelihood for continuous part, that I am able to estimate in INLA, fit data a bit worse, so these models are not of great interest for me).

Many thanks in advance,
